The soak uses the actual App/Game/results/editor lifecycle with explicit fixture completion state. It does **not** claim fully driven races. Canonical driving is covered by the separate launch/rewind/checkpoint/Star tests. Two headless and two native 50-cycle passes were run at successive implementation phases, each with 210 checks and zero failures. The later pair included a resource-preload proposal that was subsequently removed; the final ten-cycle native pass and model/cancellation checks exercise its removal. The independent final **200-cycle headless run passes 840 checks**. An optional native extension was stopped through its own test session after 33 cycles; it is not claimed as a completed 200-cycle native pass.

The later 50-cycle native pass holds **832-833 nodes, 758 resources and 354.38-355.13 MB Godot static memory** in comparable warm samples. The final ten-cycle pass holds **832 nodes, 757 resources and 354.29-354.37 MB** at cycles 5-8. Helper removal and concurrent source changes separate these phases; they are not an isolated paired comparison. These are engine counters, not Android PSS or GPU-driver residency. Raw per-cycle samples preserve pressure and owner-switch reductions separately.

The 200-cycle headless run holds **833 nodes and 936 resources** in matched warm samples. Godot static memory ranges **482.53-491.71 MB**, with **9.13 MB growth** from cycle 15 to 198. Pressure releases the optional Garage/images and drops static memory to about **366 MB** near the end. A separate 12-cycle probe keeps **5,143 objects** in comparable warm frames, with no outstanding work jobs. An eight-cycle version retaining only its last measurement row removes part of the drift, demonstrating harness overhead, but residual byte growth remains. This was **not** evidence of a fully explained memory plateau. The [follow-up investigation](MEMORY_DRIFT_INVESTIGATION_2026_10_26.md) identifies and fixes a 4,224-byte native leak per editor entry, attributes the large headless steps to Godot accessibility IDs, and replaces retained measurement rows with streaming output. Its 20-cycle windowed control has a 132-byte warm range. The historical 200-cycle observations remain unchanged; Android PSS/graphics and a fresh full-length acceptance run remain open.

The accepted Garage run reaches guest Account in **212 ms**, owns **three Garage viewports**, and records **24 room / 0 car-only reflection submissions** during vinyl rotation. First Garage entry measures **2.72 s** in that desktop run. Approximately **181 ms accumulated PNG/disk work runs on the worker**; readback is measured separately at **4.98 ms**. Shared-driver cache state and concurrent owner work prevent attributing all first-entry timing differences to this patch.

## Limits and remaining acceptance

- **R3 remains open for the original responsiveness target.** Cooperative stock-car construction reduces the measured longest uninterrupted slice from about 370 ms to **68-104 ms** across the final two runs, with 112-113 yields and about 1.1 s total assembly wall time. Largest cold Star App frames remain **503-569 ms**, observed **before world construction**, and warm Star frames **229-261 ms** during covered start/graphics preparation. The old 479 ms synchronous-car explanation no longer describes this revision. Whole-world packing is about 4-6 ms and warm instantiation about 1-2 ms. Further work must attribute the remaining cover/start CPU and driver costs; it must not replace the world cache speculatively. Driver caches were not reset for a matched cold-driver comparison. Godot documents that Compatibility needs actual first draws and lacks modern asynchronous pipeline precompilation: [official engine guidance](https://docs.godotengine.org/en/stable/tutorials/performance/pipeline_compilations.html).
- No physical-device attachment check or Android run was performed. Target-device frame pacing, Android PSS/graphics memory, thermal behavior and motion/touch/appearance acceptance remain unverified. Windows Vulkan Mobile is not an Android measurement.
- An isolated cover probe confirms only about 1.7 ms of UI setup and 0.6 ms of route-drawing submission in that run. Its first draw wait becomes much longer while the soak is running, so it is not a paired performance sample. It also reports four ObjectDB / one resource exit findings after its ten assertions; it is retained as a diagnostic, not a clean pass. The two Star images were recaptured by this process; the ten Storm images retain the accepted full-profile run.
- The sandbox emits the existing `Failed to read the root certificate store` startup error. The native Garage suite also records an OS clipboard-restoration failure after its actual diagnostic-copy assertions passed. Both are preserved. The cooperative install suite deliberately triggers its deadline warning. Accepted logs were inspected for separate script/runtime errors; a zero process exit alone is not treated as a pass.
- Broader `test_gameplay_f13_f16_f19_f24` retains its existing **one Forklift frozen-reference failure** (4,303 checks), reproduced in the earlier audit's isolated baseline. `test_backend_exception_recovery` retains its existing input-only direct-submit/fallback expectation failure and subsequent test-script error; the earlier isolated baseline reproduces both. The supported legacy **outbox recovery** suite passes. Neither failing assertion was weakened or converted to a pass.
- Root App, CloudTracks, Garage, handling, Rivalry and Storm sources changed concurrently. Their current changes are preserved. In particular, the later Storm driving-variety task supersedes the old 207-object/old road-part counts; this task does not edit the five source tracks or owner library files.
- No physics/config tuning, provider purchase/authentication, live backend mutation, device install, APK/AAB, deployment, merge, commit, or owner-process restart was performed. Only explicitly started fixture writer processes were killed.

